WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:05.610
A blue ribbon carries the ticket price. A
coral ribbon carries the number

00:00:05.610 --> 00:00:10.189
sold. Their lengths move together as one
quantity changes in the model.

00:00:15.650 --> 00:00:19.461
The ribbons become the sides of a
rectangle.

00:00:19.461 --> 00:00:25.653
Its area represents revenue, but changing
either side changes the area in a

00:00:25.653 --> 00:00:26.606
different way.

00:00:30.650 --> 00:00:33.930
Hold the price side briefly and extend the
quantity side.

00:00:33.930 --> 00:00:38.194
A new strip of area appears, showing one
contribution to the change in

00:00:38.194 --> 00:00:38.522
revenue.

00:00:45.650 --> 00:00:49.370
Now the price side shifts while the
current quantity remains visible.

00:00:49.370 --> 00:00:53.429
Another strip appears, showing why a
product has two contributions to its

00:00:53.429 --> 00:00:54.106
local change.

00:01:00.650 --> 00:01:04.693
A nested paper machine arrives beside the
rectangle.

00:01:04.693 --> 00:01:10.251
One rule feeds another inside the machine;
the rectangle multiplies two

00:01:10.251 --> 00:01:12.272
factors beside each other.

00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:19.449
When price and quantity both move, what is
the revenue derivative?

00:01:19.449 --> 00:01:23.939
Choose a rule that accounts for both
pieces, then check it another way.
